    {
        "content": "<p>It seems a key trick was to go through <code>Vector</code>. Does it mean there are API holes or is my statement unidiomatic?</p>",
        "id": 612346125,
        "sender_full_name": "Patrick Massot",
        "timestamp": 1784810843
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>I guess the reasoning is that you do not know in general what the i-th element of List.flatten is. But in your case all the lists in your nested list have the same size so you have an easy formula. And I suppose the easiest way to say \"all your lists have the same size\" is to go through <code>Vector</code> (and use <a href=\"https://leanprover-community.github.io/mathlib4_docs/find/?pattern=Vector.getElem_flatten#doc\">docs#Vector.getElem_flatten</a>). It is not clear to me if we want the List variant of that lemma (taking in an <code>m</code> and a proof <code>∀ l ∈ L, l.length = m</code>) or not.</p>",
        "id": 612350346,
        "sender_full_name": "Andrew Yang",
        "timestamp": 1784811533
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>It is also possible that your statement is unidiomatic and you should have been using Vector from the start but I cannot say for sure without knowing the applications.</p>",
        "id": 612351387,
        "sender_full_name": "Andrew Yang",
        "timestamp": 1784811745
    }
]